From: Wendy Labs <engineering@wendy.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: retain stored PSKs across ambiguous WPA2 handshake failures

A WPA2 handshake timeout can emit PskMismatch before the reason-15 disconnect.
Neither event proves the password is wrong. Requesting a replacement key can
leave a headless device in NO_SECRETS, blocking unattended reconnection.

For infrastructure WPA-PSK with matching nonempty system-owned stored/applied
keys, retain credentials on either notification while the association timer
runs. Cover explicit connection activation as well as autoconnect. Repeated
events do not reset that timer or consume a one-event allowance. PskMismatch
has no disconnect reason, so its guard necessarily also covers genuinely
wrong saved passwords; WPA authentication is still required for success.

On expiry, preserve the upstream cached-secret retry path, including profiles
without timestamp history after OTA. Allow at most two such retries per
activation request (three association windows), subject to any smaller NM
auth-retries budget. This bounds wrong-key activation even with the distro's
auth-retries=0. Fail with SUPPLICANT_TIMEOUT and a diagnostic, leaving normal
autoconnect backoff/retry policy in charge. Preserve SSID_NOT_FOUND when the
network was not seen. Cancellation does not start recovery.

This policy intentionally includes newly saved system-owned passwords: a bad
one fails within the bounded windows and must be edited/replaced by the caller.
Agent-owned, unsaved, changed applied keys, other security/modes and disconnect
reasons other than remote reason 15 retain upstream behavior. This addresses
false credential rejection, not the underlying radio/handshake fault.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/src/core/devices/wifi/nm-device-wifi.c
+++ b/src/core/devices/wifi/nm-device-wifi.c
@@ -2392,6 +2392,38 @@
     return FALSE;
 }

+/* A supplicant PSK-mismatch notification is a handshake heuristic, not proof
+ * that a stored key is wrong. In particular it can precede reason 15, without
+ * carrying the reason itself. Apply the same credential policy to both paths. */
+#define STORED_PSK_TIMEOUT_RETRIES "nm-wifi-stored-psk-timeout-retries"
+#define STORED_PSK_MAX_TIMEOUT_RETRIES 2
+
+static gboolean
+has_matching_stored_psk(NMDeviceWifi *self, NMActRequest *req)
+{
+    NMSettingsConnection      *settings;
+    NMSettingWirelessSecurity *stored, *applied;
+    const char                *psk;
+
+    if (NM_DEVICE_WIFI_GET_PRIVATE(self)->mode != _NM_802_11_MODE_INFRA)
+        return FALSE;
+
+    settings = nm_act_request_get_settings_connection(req);
+    stored = nm_connection_get_setting_wireless_security(
+        nm_settings_connection_get_connection(settings));
+    applied = nm_connection_get_setting_wireless_security(
+        nm_act_request_get_applied_connection(req));
+    if (!stored || !applied
+        || g_strcmp0(nm_setting_wireless_security_get_key_mgmt(stored), "wpa-psk") != 0
+        || g_strcmp0(nm_setting_wireless_security_get_key_mgmt(applied), "wpa-psk") != 0
+        || nm_setting_wireless_security_get_psk_flags(stored) != NM_SETTING_SECRET_FLAG_NONE
+        || nm_setting_wireless_security_get_psk_flags(applied) != NM_SETTING_SECRET_FLAG_NONE)
+        return FALSE;
+    psk = nm_setting_wireless_security_get_psk(stored);
+    return psk && psk[0]
+           && g_strcmp0(psk, nm_setting_wireless_security_get_psk(applied)) == 0;
+}
